<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Belli</h1><p class="st-meta">400 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.) · capital Segeda</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Celtiberian people of Segeda, whose new city walls provoked the Roman war of 153 BCE and so moved the start of the Roman year to January.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Belli lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalon">Jalón</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huerva">Huerva</a> valleys of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> province from about the 4th century BCE, part of the wider Celtiberian world. Their towns included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segeda">Segeda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/contrebia-belaisca">Contrebia Belaisca</a>, the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botorrita">Botorrita</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived in walled towns with councils of elders and magistrates. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botorrita">Botorrita</a> bronzes, the longest texts in the Celtiberian language, come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/contrebia-belaisca">Contrebia Belaisca</a> and record local regulations, while a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> bronze from the same site records a Roman arbitration of 87 BCE. Their economy rested on farming, herding and metalworking.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 154 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segeda">Segeda</a> enlarged its walls, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> treated as a breach of treaty. To send an army quickly, Rome began the consular year on 1 January 153 BCE. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carus-of-segeda">Carus of Segeda</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians</a> defeated the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fulvius-nobilior">Nobilior</a>, but after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a> fell in 133 BCE the Belli were absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/belli.jpg" alt="Belli" width="320" height="175"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Botorrita_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Segeda</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Carus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Celtiberian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Belaiscom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-266&amp;focus=belli" aria-label="Belli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4643 705 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Belli lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4963 961L5031 967"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5031 967h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4963 961h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4963 961h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Segeda. <a href="/atlas?year=-266&amp;focus=belli">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

